e of resources in  a moment but um  mr javid will be talking to the press  probably within the next  hour or so what's the most important  thing you want to hear from him this  morning  well what i want to hear from him is one  that he understands  the workforce because the health service  cannot survive without its workforce he  needs to understand the pressures we  feel  he needs to understand the fact that we  need to have a proper rest  and to recover before the onslaught of  the recovery of the backlog of care  that's going to need to be delivered and  he needs to value us  and he will have to also address uh the  issues around pay and  the uh derisory one percent uplift that  has you know uh distressed  a lot of the workforce in terms of not  feeling valued but the other thing he's  going to logistically need to deliver on  is the vaccination program because we  are still in the midst of a pandemic  we have spiraling uh numbers of cases  now uh over  over 18 000 yesterday uh new cases we  are actually seeing increases in  hospitalization not at the level of  before  but it is impacting on hospitals uh at  the moment  and and he's going to have to get up to  speed and make sure that the adult  population  tens of millions of which have not yet  been vaccinated get vaccinated in the  coming  weeks it's a huge challenge let's let's  take a couple of those points  um the backlog other than saying he  recognizes how hard everybody's  worked the problems of morale  outlining the numbers involved in what  does he have  to do what do you want him to say he's  going to do  to fix that so what he needs to do  is first of all retain the workforce uh  he needs to  show he needs us he's need to speak to  the british medical association the  royal economy  what signal what signal would convince  you that that's happening  okay well one would be to be honest with  the public of the scale of work so that  he doesn't just put arbitrary targets  that we just somehow can magic up  delivering care  and clear the backlog in the next few  months that just isn't going to happen  he needs to make sure that there are  issues such as rest facilities  40 percent of doctors say they don't  even have a place to rest  uh in hospital he needs to make sure  that we can take leave we can  uh recover and then that we have a  prioritization program  where those patients who need to be seen  first are seen first  and that he puts in place the logistics  to make that happen  he needs to also make sure that we are  valued both in terms of  uh the terms and conditions we work in  but also pay  and he needs to retain the workforce and  also i should mention  uh do much more with the retired  workforce who are willing to come and  help  the government did not avail of the  retired workforce last year  they need to do that now especially at  this critical time  that's interesting because i was going  to say you know we want rest  we want recognition of the pressure that  people have been under  which sounds to me like you need more  doctors  like tomorrow but you can't train them  in less than seven years  so would uh him saying what i'm going to  do  is i'm going to pay and provide  incentives  for doctors to come out of retirement be  the sort of thing that you're looking  for  well you see i mean obviously retired  doctors can't do you know they've  retired but  large numbers have actually retired in  the last two years and so they can  certainly bring their skills  uh for example in terms of the huge  backlog they can be  uh looking at the prioritization of  patients they can actually be providing  a lot of the support by phone  and video consultations for in in  primary care for example  but you're absolutely right ultimately  we have been running a health service  that are tens of thousands of doctors  short and tens of thousands of nurses  short  and that recruitment won't be able to be  delivered immediately  but obviously what he has to commit to  is a 